# LDAP Service

This service is a machine-to-machine LDAP access layer in front of two independent on-prem LDAP sources.

- Internal LDAP source: student, employee, retire
- External LDAP source: cooperator, alumni
- Fan-out strategy: search internal first, then external if not found

The goal is to provide stable HTTP APIs for lookup, authentication, and attribute modification without exposing LDAP implementation details to callers.

See [Deploying](#deploying) for CI/CD triggers, required GitHub variables, environment gates, and rollback.

## What Each Endpoint Means

| Method | Path | Meaning | API Key Required |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GET | /healthz | Liveness probe. Process is up. Does not check LDAP connectivity. | No |
| GET | /readyz | Readiness probe. Verifies both LDAP sources are reachable. | No |
| POST | /api/v1/ldap/lookup | Look up one account and selected attributes by username. | Yes |
| POST | /api/v1/ldap/lookup/batch | Look up multiple accounts in one request. | Yes |
| POST | /api/v1/ldap/authenticate | Verify username/password by search-then-bind on the matching LDAP source. | Yes |
| POST | /api/v1/ldap/modify | Replace one or more attributes on a single account (write). | Yes |

Function mapping in code:

- HandleHealthz -> GET /healthz
- HandleReadyz -> GET /readyz
- HandleLookup -> POST /api/v1/ldap/lookup
- HandleBatchLookup -> POST /api/v1/ldap/lookup/batch
- HandleAuthenticate -> POST /api/v1/ldap/authenticate
- HandleModify -> POST /api/v1/ldap/modify

## Authentication and Headers

Protected API routes under /api/v1/ldap/* require this header:

- X-Api-Key: your-service-api-key

Health endpoints (/healthz and /readyz) do not require API keys.

## Lookup Data Model

Successful lookup results return an account object with:

- dn: LDAP distinguished name
- uid: username/uid
- source: LDAP source label, either internal or external
- attributes: selected LDAP attributes as key/value pairs

The source field tells callers where the account came from.

## Allowed Lookup Attributes

Only whitelisted attributes are accepted:

- cn
- uid
- sn
- givenName
- fullName
- initials
- dept
- departmentNumber
- employeeStatus
- title
- ou
- mobile
- mail
- Alternate-Email
- birthday
- description
- disable
- idno
- originEmail

Matching is exact-string and case-sensitive. Legacy lowercase or alternate spellings (for example `fullname`, `alternative-mail`, `deptCode`) are rejected.

Requests outside this whitelist return a 400 problem response.

The whitelist is global for all authenticated API consumers. There is no per-service attribute ACL in the current MVP.

## Endpoint Contracts

### 1) GET /healthz

Meaning:

- Checks if the HTTP service process is alive.
- Does not verify LDAP servers.

Success response:

```json
{"status":"ok"}
```

### 2) GET /readyz

Meaning:

- Checks whether both LDAP sources are reachable.

Success response (200):

```json
{"status":"ready"}
```

Failure response (503, application/problem+json):

```json
{
	"type": "/problems/service-unavailable",
	"title": "Service unavailable",
	"status": 503,
	"detail": "ldap service unavailable"
}
```

### 3) POST /api/v1/ldap/lookup

Meaning:

- Finds one account by username.
- Returns only requested allowed attributes.

Request body:

```json
{
	"username": "110550001",
	"attributes": ["cn", "mail", "ou"]
}
```

Success response (200):

```json
{
	"dn": "uid=110550001,ou=student,o=nycu",
	"uid": "110550001",
	"source": "internal",
	"attributes": {
		"cn": "Student User",
		"mail": "student@example.edu.tw",
		"ou": "student"
	}
}
```

Common errors:

- 400 /problems/invalid-request
- 400 /problems/invalid-username
- 400 /problems/attribute-not-allowed
- 404 /problems/not-found
- 503 /problems/service-unavailable

### 4) POST /api/v1/ldap/lookup/batch

Meaning:

- Performs independent fan-out lookup for each username.
- Returns found accounts and a separate not_found list.

Request body:

```json
{
	"usernames": ["110550001", "alumni@example.com"],
	"attributes": ["cn", "mail"]
}
```

Success response (200):

```json
{
	"accounts": [
		{
			"dn": "uid=110550001,ou=student,o=nycu",
			"uid": "110550001",
			"source": "internal",
			"attributes": {
				"cn": "Student User",
				"mail": "student@example.edu.tw"
			}
		},
		{
			"dn": "uid=alumni@example.com,ou=alumni,o=nycu",
			"uid": "alumni@example.com",
			"source": "external",
			"attributes": {
				"cn": "Alumni User",
				"mail": "alumni@example.com"
			}
		}
	],
	"not_found": []
}
```

Note:

- not_found is an empty array when all usernames are found.

### 5) POST /api/v1/ldap/authenticate

Meaning:

- Verifies username and password via LDAP bind on the source where user is found.
- Uses generic failure semantics to avoid account existence leakage.

Request body:

```json
{
	"username": "110550001",
	"password": "plain-text-password"
}
```

Success response (200):

```json
{"authenticated":true}
```

Failure response (401, always generic):

```json
{
	"type": "/problems/authentication-failed",
	"title": "Authentication failed",
	"status": 401,
	"detail": "authentication failed"
}
```

Security notes:

- Passwords are never returned.
- Failure responses do not reveal whether the user exists.
- Username-based rate limiting applies to this endpoint.

### 6) POST /api/v1/ldap/modify

Meaning:

- Replaces one or more attributes on a single account (write operation).
- The subject account is located by fan-out (internal first, then external).
- Each attribute is applied with the LDAP `Replace` modification op (existing values are overwritten).
- Only the four attributes listed in the request body schema below are accepted as write targets. This is intentionally narrower than the lookup whitelist.

Writable attributes (request body keys):

| Key | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `disable` | Account disable flag. Must be the string `"0"` or `"1"`. |
| `userpassword` | New password. Plaintext is the recommended shape — OpenLDAP applies its configured `password-hash` directive and `ppolicy` overlay (history + quality). Pre-hashed values of the form `{SCHEME}…` (e.g. `{SSHA}`, `{ARGON2}`) are also accepted and forwarded verbatim for admin-reset / migration tooling. |
| `altemate-email` | Personal alternate email. Note: the misspelling is intentional and matches the production directory schema; do not "correct" it. |
| `temppassword` | Temporary password (free-form string). |

Any subset of the four keys may be provided; omitted keys are left untouched. At least one key must be present.

Request body:

```json
{
	"subject_id": "0856001",
	"attrs": {
		"disable": "0",
		"userpassword": "Correct-Horse-Battery-Staple",
		"altemate-email": "user@example.com",
		"temppassword": "NTLM:deadbeef"
	}
}
```

Success response (200):

```json
{
	"modified": ["disable", "userpassword", "altemate-email", "temppassword"]
}
```

- `modified` lists the attribute keys that were applied, in the canonical wire order shown above. The key spellings (including `altemate-email`) are preserved verbatim.

Common errors:

| Status | Type | Triggered by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 400 | /problems/invalid-request | Missing/empty `subject_id`, no attrs supplied, or malformed JSON. |
| 400 | /problems/invalid-username | `subject_id` fails username validation (regex, length, injection vectors). |
| 400 | /problems/invalid-attr-value | `disable` not in {`"0"`,`"1"`}, or plaintext `userpassword` is empty, longer than 256 bytes, or contains NUL / C0 control characters / DEL. |
| 401 | /problems/unauthorized | Missing or invalid `X-Api-Key`. |
| 404 | /problems/not-found | Subject not found in either LDAP source. |
| 409 | /problems/schema-violation | LDAP server rejected the modification (schema or constraint violation, including ppolicy quality / history checks on plaintext passwords). |
| 500 | /problems/internal-error | Both LDAP sources unreachable, or the modify carries `userpassword` and the LDAP connection is not TLS (the service refuses to send a password in the clear). |

Security notes:

- **TLS is mandatory for password writes.** If the configured LDAP connection is not TLS, requests carrying `userpassword` are refused with 500. Other attribute writes still succeed.
- **The service never hashes.** Plaintext is forwarded byte-for-byte; slapd's `password-hash` directive decides the storage scheme. This lets `ppolicy` enforce `pwdInHistory` and `pwdCheckQuality`, which client-side hashing breaks.
- **`{scheme}` pass-through skips ppolicy quality checks** by design (slapd cannot inspect a pre-hashed value). Use it only for admin-reset flows that intentionally bypass quality rules.
- The Problem Details `detail` field never contains the submitted `userpassword` value (nor any prefix, suffix, or length of it).
- The subject is located by search-then-modify on a single pooled connection; the bind DN must have write ACLs on the target subtree.
- Schema-violation responses are authoritative and do not fall over to the other LDAP source.

## Error Response Format

Error responses use RFC 7807 with content type application/problem+json.

Typical shape:

```json
{
	"type": "/problems/invalid-request",
	"title": "Invalid request",
	"status": 400,
	"detail": "invalid request body",
	"instance": "req-..."
}
```

## Quick Start (curl)

Set these shell variables first:

```bash
export BASE_URL="http://localhost:8080"
export API_KEY="replace-with-valid-api-key"
```

1. Check service liveness:

```bash
curl -sS "$BASE_URL/healthz"
```

2. Check LDAP readiness:

```bash
curl -sS "$BASE_URL/readyz"
```

3. Single lookup:

```bash
curl -sS -X POST "$BASE_URL/api/v1/ldap/lookup" \
	-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
	-H "X-Api-Key: $API_KEY" \
	-d '{"username":"110550001","attributes":["cn","mail","ou"]}'
```

4. Batch lookup:

```bash
curl -sS -X POST "$BASE_URL/api/v1/ldap/lookup/batch" \
	-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
	-H "X-Api-Key: $API_KEY" \
	-d '{"usernames":["110550001","alumni@example.com"],"attributes":["cn","mail"]}'
```

5. Authenticate:

```bash
curl -sS -X POST "$BASE_URL/api/v1/ldap/authenticate" \
	-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
	-H "X-Api-Key: $API_KEY" \
	-d '{"username":"110550001","password":"your-password"}'
```

6. Modify attributes (write):

```bash
curl -sS -X POST "$BASE_URL/api/v1/ldap/modify" \
	-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
	-H "X-Api-Key: $API_KEY" \
	-d '{
	  "subject_id": "0856001",
	  "attrs": {
	    "disable": "0",
	    "userpassword": "Correct-Horse-Battery-Staple",
	    "altemate-email": "user@example.com"
	  }
	}'
```

## Integration Notes

- This service is intended for backend service-to-service usage.
- Callers should treat all non-2xx responses as authoritative and inspect RFC 7807 fields.
- For authentication, do not branch behavior on failure detail; failures are intentionally generic.

## Deploying

Trigger model:

```text
merge to main -> build (git-<sha>) -> deploy-staging -> prod approval gate -> deploy-prod
```

- Automatic deploy chain: `push` to `main` runs build once, deploys to staging, then waits for reviewer approval before prod.
- Manual deploy: `workflow_dispatch` on `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` with `environment=staging|prod` deploys the selected ref.
- `workflow_dispatch` does not update `staging-latest` or `prod-latest`; only successful `main` chain deploys update those mutable tags.

Required GitHub repository variables:

| Variable | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `AZURE_TENANT_ID` | Microsoft Entra tenant ID used by OIDC login |
| `AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID` | Azure subscription ID used by OIDC login |
| `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` | Client ID of user-assigned managed identity federated to this repo |
| `ACR_NAME` | Shared Azure Container Registry name (without `.azurecr.io`) |
| `ACA_RESOURCE_GROUP_STAGING` | Resource group containing `ldap-service-staging` |
| `ACA_RESOURCE_GROUP_PROD` | Resource group containing `ldap-service-prod` |
| `ACA_APP_NAME_STAGING` | Staging Container App name (expected: `ldap-service-staging`) |
| `ACA_APP_NAME_PROD` | Production Container App name (expected: `ldap-service-prod`) |

GitHub Environments:

- `staging`: no protection rules required.
- `prod`: must have at least one required reviewer. The `deploy-prod` job will pause for approval in this environment.

Rollback:

```bash
gh workflow run deploy.yml -f environment=prod --ref <prior-good-sha>
```

Runtime configuration note:

- Runtime secrets (API key and LDAP bind credentials) are configured on each Azure Container App and are not stored in workflow files.